{"page":441,"blocks":[{"x":0.62869,"y":0.05257,"w":0.32283,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"7.1 The Logarithm Defined as an Integral 427"},{"x":0.3634,"y":0.09332,"w":0.59309,"h":0.06026,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"The graph of y = logax can be obtained by reflecting the graph of y = ax across the 45° line y = x (Figure 7.4). When a = e, we have logex = inverse of ex = ln x. Since logax and ax are inverses of one another, composing them in either order gives the identity function."},{"x":0.22882,"y":0.10001,"w":0.03514,"h":0.01217,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = 2x"},{"x":0.28008,"y":0.12997,"w":0.02907,"h":0.01076,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = x"},{"x":0.26754,"y":0.18049,"w":0.05209,"h":0.01268,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = log2x"},{"x":0.15847,"y":0.18103,"w":0.00654,"h":0.02174,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"1 2"},{"x":0.39366,"y":0.19621,"w":0.2425,"h":0.01573,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Inverse Equations for ax and logax"},{"x":0.6001,"y":0.2156,"w":0.14826,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"alogax = x (x 7 0)"},{"x":0.15847,"y":0.21791,"w":0.0524,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"0 1 2"},{"x":0.58063,"y":0.24235,"w":0.15445,"h":0.01573,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"loga(ax) = x (all x)"},{"x":0.08857,"y":0.28991,"w":0.23335,"h":0.02951,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 7.4 The graph of 2x and its inverse, log2x."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.29409,"w":0.59302,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"As stated in Section 1.6, the function loga x is just a numerical multiple of ln x. We see this from the following derivation:"},{"x":0.56441,"y":0.33947,"w":0.22868,"h":0.01601,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"y = loga x Defining equation for y"},{"x":0.55847,"y":0.35801,"w":0.21279,"h":0.01793,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"ay = x Equivalent equation"},{"x":0.10738,"y":0.37656,"w":0.19356,"h":0.02814,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"TABLE 7.2 Rules for base a logarithms"},{"x":0.54172,"y":0.38103,"w":0.25455,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"ln ay = ln x Natural log of both sides"},{"x":0.53541,"y":0.40853,"w":0.28956,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"y ln a = ln x Algebra Rule 4 for natural log"},{"x":0.11392,"y":0.41738,"w":0.18981,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"For any numbers x 7 0 and"},{"x":0.56441,"y":0.42885,"w":0.05615,"h":0.02,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = ln x"},{"x":0.11527,"y":0.43529,"w":0.0451,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y 7 0,"},{"x":0.59607,"y":0.43796,"w":0.13129,"h":0.01818,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"ln a Solve for y."},{"x":0.11392,"y":0.45875,"w":0.19375,"h":0.03223,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"1. Product Rule: logaxy = logax + logay"},{"x":0.53354,"y":0.46338,"w":0.08702,"h":0.02254,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"loga x = ln x"},{"x":0.59607,"y":0.4725,"w":0.15404,"h":0.01818,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"ln a Substitute for y."},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.50511,"w":0.5931,"h":0.0435,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"It then follows easily that the arithmetic rules satisfied by logax are the same as the ones for ln x. These rules, given in Table 7.2, can be proved by dividing the corresponding rules for the natural logarithm function by ln a. For example,"},{"x":0.11392,"y":0.50607,"w":0.12541,"h":0.0128,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"2. Quotient Rule:"},{"x":0.14337,"y":0.52062,"w":0.03623,"h":0.02151,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"logax"},{"x":0.17234,"y":0.52715,"w":0.12828,"h":0.01764,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"y = logax - logay"},{"x":0.11393,"y":0.56234,"w":0.13994,"h":0.0128,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"3. Reciprocal Rule:"},{"x":0.45731,"y":0.56583,"w":0.36874,"h":0.03195,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"ln xy = ln x + ln y Rule 1 for natural logarithms c ln xy"},{"x":0.14337,"y":0.57835,"w":0.03715,"h":0.02133,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"loga1"},{"x":0.17281,"y":0.5847,"w":0.07955,"h":0.01764,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = -logay"},{"x":0.51303,"y":0.58498,"w":0.07392,"h":0.02975,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ln a + ln y"},{"x":0.46045,"y":0.58744,"w":0.07707,"h":0.02729,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ln a = ln x"},{"x":0.56246,"y":0.59602,"w":0.22041,"h":0.01871,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"ln a c divided by ln a c"},{"x":0.11393,"y":0.61478,"w":0.14269,"h":0.03223,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"4. Power Rule: logaxy = y logax"},{"x":0.44525,"y":0.61955,"w":0.412,"h":0.01498,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"logaxy = logax + logay. c gives Rule 1 for base a logarithms."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.66463,"w":0.30694,"h":0.01619,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Derivatives and Integrals Involving logax"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.68927,"w":0.593,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"To find derivatives or integrals involving base a logarithms, we convert them to natural logarithms. If u is a positive differentiable function of x, then"},{"x":0.57218,"y":0.72745,"w":0.0531,"h":0.0305,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dx aln u"},{"x":0.60035,"y":0.72745,"w":0.08499,"h":0.03052,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"ln ab = 1 ln a"},{"x":0.68758,"y":0.72889,"w":0.11998,"h":0.02967,"fs":0.01918,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"d dx(lnu) = 1 ln a # 1"},{"x":0.48224,"y":0.73067,"w":0.10174,"h":0.02728,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"d dx(logau) = d"},{"x":0.80981,"y":0.73068,"w":0.02213,"h":0.02727,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"du dx."},{"x":0.5807,"y":0.81291,"w":0.13411,"h":0.02967,"fs":0.01918,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"d dx(logau) = 1 ln a # 1"},{"x":0.71706,"y":0.81469,"w":0.01634,"h":0.02727,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"du dx"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.88621,"w":0.43228,"h":0.01449,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 2 We illustrate the derivative and integral results."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.90578,"w":0.46296,"h":0.02967,"fs":0.01918,"n":11,"k":0,"t":"(a) d dxlog10(3x + 1) = 1 ln10 # 1 3x + 1 d dx(3x + 1) = 3 (ln10)(3x + 1)"}]}